This is the last week of Module 2. Over the past three weeks you've built a database explorer, a photo essay, and an interactive map. Today, you're building something for yourself — a portfolio site that showcases your work.

Unlike previous weeks, this class is mostly workshop time. I'll walk you through the scaffolding — multi-page routing, navigation, and layout — and then you'll spend the rest of the session building your own site. Ben Jay and I will be circulating to help.

## Part 1: What makes a good portfolio?

Before we code anything, let's look at how working journalists present their work online.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-12/portfolio-examples.png"
  alt="A grid of four journalist portfolio sites showing different approaches — minimal text-based lists, card grids with thumbnails, full-width featured projects, and magazine-style layouts"
  chromeTitle="Portfolio Examples"
  chromeUrl="various"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

A few things the best portfolio sites have in common:

**They lead with the work.** The homepage shows projects, not a biography. Your about page exists for people who want to know more, but most visitors are there to see what you've made.

**They're simple.** A portfolio doesn't need animations, parallax scrolling, or fancy transitions. It needs clear navigation, readable text, and images that load fast. The work is the design.

**They're current.** A portfolio with nothing newer than 2023 sends a signal, and not a good one. Three to five strong, recent pieces are better than a dozen mediocre ones.

**They work on phones.** Editors, recruiters, and classmates will open your link on their phone. If the layout breaks at 375 pixels wide, you've lost them.

Here are a few portfolio sites from journalists and developers worth studying. Look at how they organize their work, what they put on the homepage, and how much (or little) text they include:

- [Allison McCartney](https://allisonmccartney.com/) — one of our guest speakers this semester
- [Manas Sharma](https://manassharma.me/) — recent CUNY graduate
- [Andrew Briz](https://briz.cc/) — another guest speaker
- [Amelia Wattenberger](https://wattenberger.com/) — data visualization designer

Don't try to copy these. Look at them for structure and inspiration, then build something that fits your own work and personality.

## Part 2: Multi-page routing

Every portfolio site you just looked at has more than one page — typically a homepage, individual project pages, and an about page. Up to now, most of our projects have lived on a single page. Let's change that.

You already encountered SvelteKit's routing system in the database explorer, when we created detail pages at `/building/[id]`. The portfolio uses the same principle, but with fixed routes instead of dynamic ones.

### Set up your project

Create a new repo from the class template called `my-portfolio`. Clone it, install dependencies, and start the dev server. You know the drill.

### Creating pages

In SvelteKit, every folder inside `src/routes/` that contains a `+page.svelte` file becomes a page on your site. Here's the structure we're going to build:

```
src/routes/
  +page.svelte          → yoursite.com/           (homepage)
  +layout.svelte        → (wraps every page)
  about/
    +page.svelte        → yoursite.com/about      (about page)
  projects/
    housing/
      +page.svelte      → yoursite.com/projects/housing
    parks/
      +page.svelte      → yoursite.com/projects/parks
    shootings/
      +page.svelte      → yoursite.com/projects/shootings
```

Create these folders and files now. Each `+page.svelte` can start with a simple placeholder:

For `src/routes/about/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<h1>About</h1>
<p>This page is about me.</p>
```

For `src/routes/projects/housing/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<h1>Bronx Housing Violations</h1>
<p>My database explorer project.</p>
```

Do the same for your other project pages. Use whatever project names match your actual work from this semester.

Save the files and test the URLs in your browser. Visit [localhost:5173/about](http://localhost:5173/about) and [localhost:5173/projects/housing](http://localhost:5173/projects/housing). Each should show the placeholder content.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-12/placeholder-pages.png"
  alt="A browser showing a simple 'About' heading and placeholder text at the /about URL"
  chromeTitle="About"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173/about"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

That's all routing is in SvelteKit — folders and files. No configuration, no router library, no JSON manifest. If the folder exists and has a `+page.svelte`, it's a page.

## Part 3: Shared navigation

Right now there's no way to move between pages without editing the URL by hand. We need a navigation bar that appears on every page.

This is what `+layout.svelte` is for. You've seen this file in the template — it wraps every page. Whatever we put in the layout appears on all pages. That makes it the right place for navigation.

### Building a Nav component

Create `src/lib/components/Nav.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
</script>

<nav>
  <a href="{base}/" class:active={page.url.pathname === `${base}/`}>
    Work
  </a>
  <a href="{base}/about" class:active={page.url.pathname === `${base}/about`}>
    About
  </a>
</nav>

<style>
  nav {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-light-gray);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  a {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  a:hover {
    color: var(--color-black);
  }

  a.active {
    color: var(--color-black);
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding-bottom: 0.25rem;
  }
</style>
```

Two new imports here. The `page` object from `$app/state` gives you information about the current page, including its URL. We use `page.url.pathname` to check which page the user is on.

The `class:active` syntax is a Svelte shorthand. `class:active={condition}` adds the CSS class `active` to the element when the condition is true. So when the current URL matches the link's `href`, that link gets the `active` class and its underline turns on.

### Adding it to the layout

Open `src/routes/+layout.svelte`. This file already exists in the template. Replace its content with:

```svelte
<script>
  import Nav from '$lib/components/Nav.svelte';
  let { children } = $props();
</script>

<div class="site">
  <header>
    <h1 class="site-name"><a href="/">Your Name</a></h1>
    <Nav />
  </header>
  <main>
    {@render children()}
  </main>
</div>

<style>
  .site {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .site-name {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
  }

  .site-name a {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--color-black);
  }

  main {
    min-height: 60vh;
  }
</style>
```

Replace "Your Name" with your actual name.

Save and check your browser. Every page should now have your name at the top and a navigation bar with "Work" and "About" links. Click between them — the active state should follow you.

