## Part 4: The homepage

The homepage is the first thing anyone sees. It should show your projects — not a wall of text about yourself, not a welcome message, just the work.

We'll build it as a grid of project cards, each linking to a project page. Open `src/routes/+page.svelte` and replace the content:

```svelte
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';

  const projects = [
    {
      title: 'Bronx Housing Violations',
      description: 'A searchable database of buildings with hazardous housing code violations in the Bronx.',
      url: `${base}/projects/housing`,
      image: '/thumbnails/housing.jpg'
    },
    {
      title: 'Harlem, 1943',
      description: 'A photo essay built from Gordon Parks\'s public domain photographs of Harlem life.',
      url: `${base}/projects/parks`,
      image: '/thumbnails/parks.jpg'
    },
    {
      title: 'NYC Shooting Incidents',
      description: 'An interactive map of shooting incidents across New York City, filterable by borough and severity.',
      url: `${base}/projects/shootings`,
      image: '/thumbnails/shootings.jpg'
    }
  ];
</script>

<div class="projects">
  {#each projects as project}
    <a class="project-card" href={project.url}>
      <img src={project.image} alt={project.title} />
      <h2>{project.title}</h2>
      <p>{project.description}</p>
    </a>
  {/each}
</div>

<style>
  .projects {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: 2rem;
  }

  .project-card {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    display: block;
  }

  .project-card img {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .project-card h2 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
  }

  .project-card p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .project-card:hover img {
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
</style>
```

For the thumbnail images, take a screenshot of each of your projects and save them to `static/thumbnails/`. They don't need to be high-resolution — a browser screenshot cropped to a 16:9 rectangle works fine. If you don't have screenshots ready, use placeholder images for now and replace them later.

The CSS Grid layout with `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr))` is a responsive pattern worth understanding. It says: "Fill the row with as many columns as will fit, where each column is at least 300 pixels wide and can stretch to take equal space." On a wide screen, you'll see two or three cards per row. On a phone, it collapses to a single column. No media queries needed.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-12/homepage-grid.png"
  alt="A portfolio homepage showing three project cards in a responsive grid, each with a thumbnail image, title, and short description"
  chromeTitle="Your Name"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

### Customizing your project list

Update the `projects` array to reflect your actual work. If you have projects from outside this class — from another course, a personal project, a freelance assignment — include them. Three to five projects is a good target. More than that and the page starts to feel cluttered. Less and it feels empty.

Write each description as a single sentence that tells the reader what the project is, not what tools you used. "A searchable database of Bronx housing violations" is better than "A SvelteKit project using JSON data and dynamic routes."

## Part 5: The about page

The about page is where you tell your story. Keep it short. An editor or recruiter spending 30 seconds on your site wants to know: Who are you? What do you do? How do I contact you?

Open `src/routes/about/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<div class="about">
  <img class="headshot" src="/headshot.jpg" alt="Your Name" />

  <h1>About me</h1>

  <p>
    I'm a graduate student at the Craig Newmark Graduate School
    of Journalism at CUNY, focusing on data and interactive journalism.
    I'm interested in housing, public safety, and making public records
    accessible to the communities they describe.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before CUNY, I [your background]. I'm currently looking for
    [internship/job/freelance] opportunities in [your area of interest].
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>

  <p>
    Email: <a href="mailto:you@email.com">you@email.com</a><br>
    GitHub: <a href="https://github.com/yourusername" target="_blank">github.com/yourusername</a><br>
    LinkedIn: <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/yourusername" target="_blank">linkedin.com/in/yourusername</a>
  </p>
</div>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .headshot {
    width: 150px;
    height: 150px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  p {
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
```

Save a headshot photo to `static/headshot.jpg`. If you don't have a professional headshot, use any photo of yourself, or skip the image for now and add it later.

Rewrite the bio in your own words. First person is fine. Be direct. One or two paragraphs is enough.

