## Part 6: Project pages

Each project page in your portfolio should tell the story of what you built — not reproduce the project itself. Your actual projects already live at their own GitHub Pages URLs. The portfolio page for each project is a case study: what the project is, why you made it, a screenshot or two, and a link to the live version.

Here's a template for a project page. Open `src/routes/projects/housing/+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { base } from '$app/paths';
</script>

<a href="{base}/" class="back">&larr; Back to all projects</a>

<h1>Bronx Housing Violations</h1>

<img
  class="hero"
  src="/thumbnails/housing.jpg"
  alt="Screenshot of the Bronx Housing Violations database explorer"
/>

<p>
  A searchable database of Bronx buildings with open, immediately hazardous
  housing code violations. Users can search by address, filter by zip code,
  and click into detail pages for individual buildings showing the full
  violation record and a locator map.
</p>

<p>
  Built with SvelteKit using data from the NYC Department of Housing
  Preservation and Development, pre-aggregated at the building level
  from the city's open data portal.
</p>

<p>
  <a href="https://yourusername.github.io/bronx-housing-violations/" target="_blank">
    View the live project &rarr;
  </a>
</p>

<style>
  .back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-dark-gray);
  }

  .hero {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: 4px;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  p {
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 600px;
  }
</style>
```

Create similar pages for your other projects. Swap in the right titles, descriptions, screenshots, and links. Two to three paragraphs per project is enough. Focus on what the project does and what editorial choices you made, not on the technical implementation.

## Part 7: Workshop

That's the scaffolding. You have multi-page routing, shared navigation, a homepage grid, an about page, and project page templates.

Now it's time to make it yours.

For the rest of class, build out your portfolio. Here's what to work on, roughly in order of priority:

1. **Fill in your real content.** Replace every placeholder with your actual name, bio, project descriptions, and links. Take screenshots of your live projects and save them as thumbnails.

2. **Add your projects.** Create a page for each project you want to showcase. Link to the live versions.

3. **Customize the design.** Change the colors, fonts, spacing, and layout to make the site feel like yours. This is a good time to use your AI coding assistant. Try prompts like:
   - "Change the color scheme to use dark navy and gold."
   - "Make the navigation horizontal on desktop and a hamburger menu on mobile."
   - "Add a subtle hover animation to the project cards."
   - "Make the site title use a serif font."

4. **Incorporate a technique from a previous week.** The Module 2 rubric awards a point for carrying over a technique from an earlier project. Some ideas:
   - Embed one of your interactive projects directly in a project page instead of just linking to it
   - Add a small MapLibre map to your about page showing where you're based
   - Build the project listing as a filterable grid using `$state` and `$derived`
   - Use the scroll-snap gallery pattern from the photo essay week for a project showcase

5. **Test on your phone.** Open the site on a real device. Check that the navigation works, the grid collapses to a single column, the text is readable, and nothing overflows the screen.

I'll be walking around the room. Flag me or Ben Jay if you get stuck.

### Check-in

We'll pause halfway through workshop time for a quick round of voluntary screen-shares. If you want to show your progress, share your screen and give us a 30-second tour. This is informal — it's a chance to get feedback and see what your classmates are doing.

## Part 8: Deploy and share

When you're ready, commit and push to GitHub. Watch the Actions tab to make sure the build succeeds.

Once your site is live, share the URL in the class chat. Pull up a few of your classmates' sites. See how differently everyone approached the same structure.

Your portfolio doesn't need to be finished tonight. You have a week to polish it before the homework is due. But by the end of this session, you should have a working multi-page site deployed to a live URL with at least placeholder content on every page.

## Homework

### Task 1: Complete your portfolio

Finish and polish your portfolio site. It must have:

- At least three pieces of work or projects
- Working navigation between pages
- An about page with your bio and contact information
- Screenshots or thumbnails for each project
- A live deployment on GitHub Pages

Update the content, refine the design, and test on your phone before submitting.

Deploy it to GitHub Pages and send me the link.

### Task 2: Prepare to present

Be ready to show your portfolio to the class. You should be prepared to:

- Walk us through the site on a projector or shared screen
- Explain one design choice you made and why
- Show one technique you carried over from a previous Module 2 week
- Tell us what you'd add or change if you had more time
